`useStablePd` is on by default and applies **only** to the neck and head.
CLI `-spd 0` restores the former ordinary PD on the same build — the flag was left
exactly for comparison, not as a setting. Arms are on stable PD **always**:
the muscle loop on light segments is not tuned with explicit PD.

`hipBaseAngle` is now the **world pelvis-tilt target**, not a joint angle.
The thigh holds the pelvis to vertical via `ControlHipsToWorldUpright`, so
`hipPGain`, `hipDGain`, and `crouchHipFlex` no longer affect anything. The names
were kept so the stand and Inspector do not break; when reading summaries remember
that these three fields are dead.

The pelvis sign is **not the same as the lumbar**: `+P*error − D*speed`. The lumbar
joint sits on the chest, the hip joint on the thigh, and the pelvis receives torque with
a minus sign as `connectedBody`. Both gains are positive.

The pair of base angles is linked: at knee +K the ankle sits under the pelvis if
the thigh is tilted forward by K/2. The former `10 / −8` with plus-side flexion would have
taken the ankle 21 cm behind the hip.

`lumbarPGain` / CLI `-lumbarP` — plus 2. The minuses are already in the lumbar
formula; a negative value must not be passed via CLI.

# What we reached

The damper in `JointFriction` is unconditionally stable.

On 24.08 two fixes were done in a row: knee flip and world-orientation
control of the pelvis. The flip by itself worsened push thresholds; pelvis control
restored them and also improved stance more than ever.

24.08 added a third fix: neck and head moved to world vertical.
25.08 — fourth: stable PD (SPD) on the same two joints. It closed
the chatter that started the whole effort.

**Stable PD is the current baseline.** Baseline `Trials/Baselines/stable-pd/`,
fingerprint `05db3e8e1643bf1a1af2257628b0bccb9d5e7fcd2ddafc19f12daef0fc945f87`,
runs `s_on` (stance 30 s), `son_squat`, `son_pf23` / `son_pf24`,
`son_pb23` / `son_pb24`. Next to them sit `s_off` and `soff_squat` — the same arguments
with `-spd 0`, a clean comparison on one build.

